1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,987
NASA's next Moon mission was supposed to land here.

2
00:00:03,167 --> 00:00:05,279
Now it won't even leave Earth orbit.

3
00:00:05,809 --> 00:00:08,476
In April, four astronauts flew around the

4
00:00:08,476 --> 00:00:09,777
Moon on Artemis Two.

5
00:00:09,957 --> 00:00:12,730
And the next crew's rocket won't even carry

6
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:15,504
the stage that sends Orion toward the Moon.

7
00:00:15,684 --> 00:00:16,772
In its place:

8
00:00:16,952 --> 00:00:19,042
a metal barrel with no engine.

9
00:00:19,572 --> 00:00:22,279
NASA made this change back in February, and

10
00:00:22,279 --> 00:00:24,671
in October it published the full plan:

11
00:00:24,851 --> 00:00:27,648
fourteen days in orbit, two Moon landers,

12
00:00:27,648 --> 00:00:30,582
and one test that has to work before anyone

13
00:00:30,582 --> 00:00:32,765
lands near the lunar South Pole.

14
00:00:32,945 --> 00:00:35,410
Here's what they'll actually do up there,

15
00:00:35,410 --> 00:00:38,236
and why the landing now waits for Artemis Four.

16
00:00:38,766 --> 00:00:41,113
Artemis Two's flight took ten days:

17
00:00:41,293 --> 00:00:44,443
launch on April first, a seven-hour lunar

18
00:00:44,443 --> 00:00:47,209
flyby on April sixth, splashdown off

19
00:00:47,209 --> 00:00:49,207
California on April tenth.

20
00:00:49,387 --> 00:00:51,854
But by then the plan for the next mission

21
00:00:51,854 --> 00:00:53,057
had already changed.

22
00:00:53,237 --> 00:00:56,310
On February twenty-seventh, NASA rewrote the

23
00:00:56,310 --> 00:00:58,335
order of the Artemis program.

24
00:00:58,515 --> 00:01:00,563
Artemis Three had been the landing.

25
00:01:00,743 --> 00:01:03,582
It became a test flight in low Earth orbit,

26
00:01:03,582 --> 00:01:06,355
set for twenty twenty-seven, and the first

27
00:01:06,355 --> 00:01:09,194
landing moved to Artemis Four, targeted for

28
00:01:09,194 --> 00:01:10,514
twenty twenty-eight.

29
00:01:10,694 --> 00:01:13,449
NASA also promised at least one landing

30
00:01:13,449 --> 00:01:15,003
every year after that.

31
00:01:15,183 --> 00:01:17,850
NASA's reasoning, in its own announcement:

32
00:01:18,030 --> 00:01:20,876
keep the rocket and spacecraft as close as

33
00:01:20,876 --> 00:01:23,721
possible to the version that already flew,

34
00:01:23,721 --> 00:01:26,567
fly more often, and build up step by step,

35
00:01:26,567 --> 00:01:29,006
with each mission big enough to make

36
00:01:29,006 --> 00:01:31,513
progress but not so big that it takes

37
00:01:31,513 --> 00:01:32,665
unnecessary risk.

38
00:01:32,845 --> 00:01:35,571
NASA's administrator said that's how the

39
00:01:35,571 --> 00:01:38,093
agency did it in nineteen sixty-nine.

40
00:01:38,623 --> 00:01:40,586
And this isn't a paper mission.

41
00:01:40,766 --> 00:01:43,437
The four main engines for Artemis Three

42
00:01:43,437 --> 00:01:45,971
reached Kennedy Space Center in July.

43
00:01:46,151 --> 00:01:48,000
The Orion capsule and its service module

44
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,780
were joined in late July.

45
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:53,798
On September thirtieth, technicians put the

46
00:01:53,798 --> 00:01:57,296
nose cones on the twin boosters, finishing the stack.

47
00:01:57,476 --> 00:02:00,419
More than two hundred and fifty parts from

48
00:02:00,419 --> 00:02:03,151
Artemis Two's Orion are being reused on

49
00:02:03,151 --> 00:02:06,094
later missions, and NASA says reused parts

50
00:02:06,094 --> 00:02:09,807
move some installation work up by about three months.

51
00:02:09,987 --> 00:02:12,681
Even the launch tower is being turned around

52
00:02:12,681 --> 00:02:15,619
for its next flight nearly fifty percent faster.

53
00:02:15,799 --> 00:02:17,484
And that missing upper stage?

54
00:02:17,664 --> 00:02:19,051
It isn't scrapped.

55
00:02:19,231 --> 00:02:21,827
NASA reassigned it to Artemis Four, the

56
00:02:21,827 --> 00:02:24,756
mission that actually has to reach the Moon.

57
00:02:25,286 --> 00:02:28,739
The crew is Randy Bresnik, Andre Douglas and

58
00:02:28,739 --> 00:02:32,035
Frank Rubio of NASA, and Luca Parmitano of

59
00:02:32,035 --> 00:02:34,075
the European Space Agency.

60
00:02:34,255 --> 00:02:36,603
In September they trained with Navy

61
00:02:36,603 --> 00:02:39,420
helicopter crews off San Diego, practicing

62
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:42,639
being hoisted out of the water after splashdown.

63
00:02:42,819 --> 00:02:45,076
They've drilled escaping a capsule

64
00:02:45,076 --> 00:02:47,997
underwater, trained in their orange survival

65
00:02:47,997 --> 00:02:50,786
suits, and tasted nearly two hundred items

66
00:02:50,786 --> 00:02:51,715
of space food.

67
00:02:51,895 --> 00:02:54,381
Their mission will be about two weeks long,

68
00:02:54,381 --> 00:02:56,694
and it will never get farther than a few

69
00:02:56,694 --> 00:02:58,082
hundred miles from home.

70
00:02:58,612 --> 00:03:00,831
Artemis Three needs three launches.

71
00:03:01,011 --> 00:03:03,957
First, Blue Origin launches a test version

72
00:03:03,957 --> 00:03:05,640
of its Blue Moon lander.

73
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:08,465
It can wait in orbit for up to thirty days.

74
00:03:08,645 --> 00:03:12,955
Then the crew launches on NASA's SLS rocket from Florida.

75
00:03:13,135 --> 00:03:15,804
Starship goes up later, after the crew is

76
00:03:15,804 --> 00:03:18,539
already in orbit, because it's built for a

77
00:03:18,539 --> 00:03:20,882
much shorter stay in space than Blue

78
00:03:20,882 --> 00:03:21,924
Origin's lander.

79
00:03:22,454 --> 00:03:24,011
The orbits are precise.

80
00:03:24,191 --> 00:03:27,220
The Blue Moon test article circles about two

81
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:29,354
hundred seventy-three miles up.

82
00:03:29,534 --> 00:03:32,231
Orion starts in a long, stretched orbit,

83
00:03:32,231 --> 00:03:34,995
from eighteen to more than eleven hundred

84
00:03:34,995 --> 00:03:38,771
miles, then rounds it out near three hundred forty-five.

85
00:03:38,951 --> 00:03:41,744
Starship is planned lower still, at two

86
00:03:41,744 --> 00:03:44,680
hundred six nautical miles, an orbit NASA

87
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:47,186
picked for communications coverage.

88
00:03:47,366 --> 00:03:50,481
Every number here comes from NASA's flight profile.

89
00:03:51,011 --> 00:03:52,270
First docking:

90
00:03:52,450 --> 00:03:55,436
Orion pulls up alongside the Blue Moon lander.

91
00:03:55,616 --> 00:03:58,304
While they're joined, Orion's own software

92
00:03:58,304 --> 00:03:59,712
flies both spacecraft.

93
00:03:59,892 --> 00:04:01,386
Then the hatch opens.

94
00:04:01,566 --> 00:04:03,982
Up to two astronauts, in their orange

95
00:04:03,982 --> 00:04:05,875
survival suits, float inside.

96
00:04:06,055 --> 00:04:08,917
NASA calls it its first evaluation of a

97
00:04:08,917 --> 00:04:11,559
commercial lunar lander with a crew.

98
00:04:11,739 --> 00:04:14,114
They spend about two days checking its

99
00:04:14,114 --> 00:04:16,176
cabin, controls and life support.

100
00:04:16,356 --> 00:04:18,963
Riding along is a dummy the weight of a

101
00:04:18,963 --> 00:04:21,638
spacesuit, wired with sensors, reporting

102
00:04:21,638 --> 00:04:23,844
what the cabin really feels like.

103
00:04:24,374 --> 00:04:25,675
Second docking:

104
00:04:25,855 --> 00:04:26,815
Starship.

105
00:04:26,995 --> 00:04:29,824
The test vehicle is a hundred seventy-one

106
00:04:29,824 --> 00:04:32,652
feet tall, about fifty-two meters, with a

107
00:04:32,652 --> 00:04:34,377
docking port in its nose.

108
00:04:34,557 --> 00:04:37,384
This time Orion uses its forward docking

109
00:04:37,384 --> 00:04:40,140
port and meets Starship nose to nose, a

110
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:43,108
different geometry from the first docking,

111
00:04:43,108 --> 00:04:44,946
and SpaceX flies the pair.

112
00:04:45,126 --> 00:04:48,646
It lasts up to two hours, and nobody goes inside.

113
00:04:48,826 --> 00:04:51,916
NASA and SpaceX are still deciding exactly

114
00:04:51,916 --> 00:04:55,226
which control and communication tests to run.

115
00:04:55,756 --> 00:04:58,701
After Starship, NASA has set aside up to

116
00:04:58,701 --> 00:05:00,983
five more days for extra tests:

117
00:05:01,163 --> 00:05:03,909
more checks of the crew's survival suits,

118
00:05:03,909 --> 00:05:06,320
docking sensor tests, sound and heat

119
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:09,067
measurements, and studies of how the crew

120
00:05:09,067 --> 00:05:10,741
lives and works on board.

121
00:05:10,921 --> 00:05:13,910
Then, after about fourteen days in total,

122
00:05:13,910 --> 00:05:16,898
Orion comes home and splashes down in the

123
00:05:16,898 --> 00:05:19,887
Pacific, off the coast of California, the

124
00:05:19,887 --> 00:05:23,167
same ocean where Artemis Two landed in April.

125
00:05:23,697 --> 00:05:25,937
So why spend a whole mission on this?

126
00:05:26,117 --> 00:05:29,317
NASA's Artemis program manager put it plainly:

127
00:05:29,497 --> 00:05:32,313
every piece of the mission in Earth orbit is

128
00:05:32,313 --> 00:05:35,129
designed to reduce risk before astronauts go

129
00:05:35,129 --> 00:05:36,217
back to the Moon.

130
00:05:36,397 --> 00:05:39,269
Three spacecraft on three different rockets,

131
00:05:39,269 --> 00:05:41,881
working together, which he says NASA has

132
00:05:41,881 --> 00:05:42,925
never attempted.

133
00:05:43,105 --> 00:05:46,023
And on Artemis Four, the landing depends on

134
00:05:46,023 --> 00:05:47,244
exactly this step.

135
00:05:47,424 --> 00:05:48,960
Orion can't land.

136
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:51,540
A commercial lander has to meet it, take

137
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:53,940
astronauts down near the South Pole, and

138
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:54,900
bring them back.

139
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:57,679
So the first test of this handoff happens

140
00:05:57,679 --> 00:05:59,581
close to home, in Earth orbit.

141
00:06:00,111 --> 00:06:02,244
If this sounds familiar, it should.

142
00:06:02,424 --> 00:06:05,365
In March nineteen sixty-nine, Apollo Nine

143
00:06:05,365 --> 00:06:08,163
tested the lunar module in Earth orbit.

144
00:06:08,343 --> 00:06:11,180
Four months later, Apollo Eleven landed.

145
00:06:11,710 --> 00:06:13,118
What we don't know yet:

146
00:06:13,298 --> 00:06:14,535
a launch date.

147
00:06:14,715 --> 00:06:18,321
NASA says twenty twenty-seven, and nothing more precise.

148
00:06:18,501 --> 00:06:21,509
The rocket's fueling test at the pad is still ahead.

149
00:06:21,689 --> 00:06:24,458
And NASA hasn't said which lander will carry

150
00:06:24,458 --> 00:06:26,723
the first crew down on Artemis Four.

151
00:06:27,253 --> 00:06:29,690
So the next Moon mission stays close to

152
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:30,752
home, on purpose.

153
00:06:30,932 --> 00:06:33,639
If both dockings work, the following crew

154
00:06:33,639 --> 00:06:35,092
goes all the way down.

155
00:06:35,272 --> 00:06:37,320
Artemis Four is the landing.

156
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:40,124
Artemis Three is the test it can't skip.

